\title{Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer?\\ An Empirical Investigation}

\author{
Anonymous Author(s)
}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models can achieve high task success rates yet remain behaviorally unsafe: on SafeLIBERO Level~I, OpenVLA attains 90.7\% task success but only 78.0\% collision avoidance.
This paper presents the first factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance.
Multiple conditions on OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA vary gradient flow, label content, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent: Vanilla (action loss only), Full OCC (BEV occupancy with ground-truth labels), Random OCC (random binary labels), Detach OCC (encoder gradients blocked), Detach+LN (LayerNorm replacing BatchNorm), Depth (egocentric depth prediction), Cutoff@\{15K--45K\} (gradient flow for the first $N$ of 50K steps), and Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow for the last 15K steps only).
The central finding is that the Detach OCC degradation ($-$3.4\,pp CAR vs.\ Vanilla) decomposes into two mechanistically distinct pathways: BatchNorm interference accounts for all representational drift (replacing BN with LayerNorm recovers pretrained CKA to Vanilla level and 1.9\,pp CAR; Detach+LN 0.765), while gradient flow absence costs a modest additional 1.5\,pp (within statistical uncertainty) through behavioral regularization beyond representation preservation. Gradient norms fall to 1--13\% of baseline under gradient detachment; BatchNorm interference drives representations away from pretrained features (CKA dropping to 0.31--0.54), while Detach+LN (LayerNorm substitution) fully recovers pretrained CKA despite retaining \texttt{stop\_gradient}. Full OCC and Random OCC maintain encoder gradient norms at 0.87--1.14$\times$ baseline, preserve pretrained representations (CKA $\geq$ 0.95), and CAR within 1.7\,pp of Vanilla (78.0--79.7\%).
Within the variance of our experiments, label content type does not significantly affect downstream safety metrics: structured occupancy, random binary maps, and egocentric depth yield comparable collision avoidance when gradient flow is maintained.
Cutoff@35K retains high task success (93.3\%) with CAR statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla ($-$0.1\,pp); preliminary cutoff experiments suggest that gradient flow \emph{continuity} matters more than mere existence---mid-training transitions between gradient-flow and Detach modes introduce seed-dependent outcome variability not observed under consistent training regimes.
Because the auxiliary head is discarded after training, these spatial losses add zero inference cost and require no ground-truth spatial labels at deployment.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Vision-Language-Action Models, Robot Safety, Auxiliary Supervision, Collision Avoidance}

